---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="FreightPilot AI — A Safer Freight-Booking Sandbox"
  description="How FreightPilot AI uses evidence, approval, and recovery controls to make a local freight-booking workflow inspectable."
  page={null}
  canonical="https://sajadxdp0.github.io/freightpilot-ai.html"
>
  <main id="main" tabindex="-1" class="article-main">
    <article>
      <header class="article-header">
        <p class="article-kicker">Project notes</p>
        <h1>FreightPilot AI: building a safer freight-booking sandbox</h1>
        <p>A local prototype for making freight-booking assistance inspectable before it is allowed to act.</p>
      </header>

      <section aria-labelledby="built-heading">
        <h2 id="built-heading">What was built</h2>
        <p>FreightPilot AI is a local sandbox built with FastAPI, SQLite, and a static dashboard. It takes synthetic emails and PDFs through a document-intake flow, records file hashes with SHA-256, and attaches evidence offsets to extracted shipment fields. Deterministic validation checks the candidate data, while an optional strict OpenAI-compatible adapter can provide reasoned operator review. The adapter is an integration point, not the authority for whether a draft may proceed.</p>
        <p>The point is to keep the path from a messy request to a proposed draft visible. A reviewer can inspect the supporting evidence and validation results instead of accepting an unexplained answer.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="safety-heading">
        <h2 id="safety-heading">The central safety idea</h2>
        <p>Approval is bound to one exact plan, not to a general instruction. The plan hash is tied to the target record, its before-values, and its revision. An operator first reviews the reasoned proposal; a second demo identity must then approve it within 30 minutes. If the target or revision changes, that approval cannot silently carry over.</p>
        <p>Execution is deliberately narrow: a fixed Playwright page object logs into a dedicated demo TMS through a loopback-only origin and edits only allowlisted draft fields. It does not get a general-purpose browser tool or permission to invent actions. Approval remains with people, and the software can stop rather than improvise.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="workflow-heading">
        <h2 id="workflow-heading">The local workflow</h2>
        <p>A request enters as synthetic source material. Intake produces evidence-linked fields, deterministic checks surface problems, and the adapter can explain a proposed draft for operator review. After the second approval, the worker takes an atomic execution lease and drives the known demo-TMS workflow. Per-action checkpoints and screenshots record progress, and read-back verifies the resulting state rather than treating a submitted form as success.</p>
        <p>This is a complete local loop, not a claim that real freight systems are connected.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="recovery-heading">
        <h2 id="recovery-heading">Recovery and verification</h2>
        <p>Browser automation fails in ways that ordinary request handling does not: a save can succeed after its response is lost, a layout can change, or a worker can restart between steps. FreightPilot persists its execution state, prevents overlapping workers with a lease, and reconciles the actual demo-TMS state before resuming. Reconciliation comes before retrying the remaining action.</p>
        <p>The real-browser acceptance scenarios cover the happy path, a lost save response, and a changed-layout stop followed by recovery. Controlled failure injection exercises the recovery paths. These cases test whether the system notices uncertainty and responds safely, not just whether a script can click through a page.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="practice-heading">
        <h2 id="practice-heading">Lessons from loop and Fable-inspired practice</h2>
        <p>The engineering follows small, inspectable loops: observe evidence, propose a bounded action, require approval, act through a fixed interface, then verify. Fable-inspired browser practice reinforces the value of page objects and stable action boundaries over free-form interaction. The broader lesson is that agentic convenience and operational control reinforce each other when each step has a clear precondition, checkpoint, and stop condition.</p>
        <p>Evaluation is offline and synthetic: 60 cases exercise the domain flow without presenting invented accuracy figures. The <a href="https://github.com/sajadxdp0/FreightPilot-AI/blob/main/evals/reports/report.json">repository evaluation report</a> contains the detailed results.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="limits-heading">
        <h2 id="limits-heading">Honest limitations</h2>
        <p>This is not production freight software. There is no live inbox or TMS integration, and no autonomous booking, release, payment, cancellation, deletion, or sending. Production authentication and tenancy, OCR, PostgreSQL, Redis, and Celery are outside the prototype. SQLite and synchronous execution are intentional local-prototype choices, not a production scaling design. The safety claims apply to the constrained sandbox workflow; they are not evidence of full-spec production readiness.</p>
      </section>

      <p class="article-source"><a href="https://github.com/sajadxdp0/FreightPilot-AI">View FreightPilot AI on GitHub</a></p>
      <p class="article-back"><a href="/projects.html">Back to Projects</a></p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
